{
  "notes": [
    "Ground truth windows come from NASA's own published .vtt captions, not from",
    "anything the pipeline produced, so the eval cannot grade itself.",
    "The Perseverance clip ships no captions, so its windows are whole-video and",
    "only test which video is retrieved, not where inside it.",
    "kind=visual_only cases are the important ones: the answer is on screen and is",
    "never spoken, so a transcript index cannot find them and a scene index must.",
    "Every semantic case names its index. Scores from different indexes are not",
    "calibrated against each other, so searching them together lets one index crowd",
    "out another: a transcript hit scoring 0.69 was absent from a 20-result",
    "multi-index search entirely. The agent queries per index and merges explicitly.",
    "cross-modal-water-evidence is the one deliberate multi-index case, and it is",
    "expected to FAIL. It documents both that crowding and the fact that collection",
    "search concentrates on whichever video is densest on the topic.",
    "top_k is 15, scaled to a 386-scene corpus. Precision is bounded by top_k when a",
    "case has one correct 10-second window, so recall and rank-1 are the real signal.",
    "No case grades a subjective label. Whether launch commentary counts as a",
    "'briefing' has no ground truth, so filters are asserted only on facts that can",
    "be checked against the footage.",
    "history-mer-watery-past is a second deliberate failure. It asks in a",
    "researcher's vocabulary ('twin rovers') for something the archive states in its",
    "own ('the first of two rovers'), and semantic search does not bridge the gap.",
    "history-mer-watery-past-expanded is the identical claim in the archive's",
    "wording and hits rank 1 at 0.78. The pair is the concrete argument for query",
    "expansion in the agent: decomposition has to produce alternate phrasings, not",
    "just sub-questions.",
    "Cases from apollo17-field-geologist onward cover the non-Mars domains added in the",
    "coverage expansion: Moon, human spaceflight, outer planets. Their windows come from",
    "the same source as the rest, NASA's published .vtt captions.",
    "aggregate-bodies-covered and filter-moon-scenes are corpus-shape tests rather than",
    "retrieval tests. They fail if the corpus drifts back to being effectively Mars-only,",
    "which is what made the spatial interface static in the first place."
  ],
